{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from the year 1152 until his death in 1187 and he held a position of immense influence within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era. His life story involves complex political maneuvering alongside military leadership against rising Muslim powers in the Levant region. He often acted as a regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not always rule with full capacity on his own. Raymond sought peace with Saladin while other factions within the kingdom pushed for aggressive war that ultimately led to disaster at Hattin.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond inherited his title after his father Raymond II was assassinated by the Order of Assassins in Tortosa. This event forced the young count to assume responsibility for a fragile state surrounded by hostile forces. He spent his early years consolidating power within his own county while building alliances with neighboring Crusader states. His marriage to Eschiva of Bures gave him control over Galilee which expanded his territorial holdings significantly. This union strengthened his political standing and made him one of the most powerful barons in the entire kingdom.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe count became regent for King Baldwin IV during the minority of the monarch and later during periods when the king was too ill to govern. Raymond faced opposition from the Courtenay family who desired power for themselves through Agnes of Courtenay and her children. These internal conflicts weakened the Christian position against external threats. Raymond advocated for diplomatic solutions with Saladin whereas the faction led by Guy of Lusignan preferred direct military confrontation. His cautious approach often put him at odds with the Knights Templar and other militant orders who sought constant warfare.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Death*\nTensions reached a breaking point when Guy of Lusignan became king and ignored the advice of Raymond regarding military strategy. The resulting campaign ended in the catastrophic Battle of Hattin where the Crusader army was destroyed and many leaders were captured or killed. Raymond managed to escape the battlefield but the loss marked the beginning of the end for the Kingdom of Jerusalem as Saladin conquered most cities shortly after. Raymond died later in 1187 possibly from illness or despair over the collapse of the Christian hold on the Holy Land. His legacy remains that of a pragmatic leader who understood the limitations of Crusader power better than his rivals did during those final critical years before the fall of Jerusalem.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -1.6927863e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0004346719}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.000111824564}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -3.5991635}, {"token": " year", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -4.649173e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -9.536748e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -1.1920936e-06}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -3.743242e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.1457695e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -7.629424e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.4107533e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.0005210207}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0009451205}, {"token": " held", "logprob": -3.0886147}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -1